  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool temperatures will continue through the\nweekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nTemperatures will remain well below normal Friday and Saturday\nwith CWA under the influence of an upr level trof. Most\nlocations will struggle to get out of the 50s the next 2 days. A\nbackdoor cold front will move into the area mid to late\nafternoon possibly bringing a few showers to eastern zones. \n\nA H5 low will drop into western New England this evening with \nbulk of measurable rainfall remaining to the west of the CWA, \nthough may see around 0.10 inches from Moosehead Lake area down \ntoward MDI as it tracks south toward Cape Cod. Lows tonight \nwill drop into the mid-30s with frost possible acrs the north. \n\nBy Saturday night, period of clearing as coastal low moves\nnortheast, and another low develops in the north. Clearing,\nalongside light winds, results in temperatures falling into the\nmid-30s throughout northern and eastern Maine, which could lead\nto frost development. Plan to protect any sensitive vegetation. Cool\ndaytime highs on Sunday, in the low-to-mid 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorms possible Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs low pressure systems approach from the northwest on Sunday \nmorning, rain showers creep into western Aroostook and northern\nSomerset, slowly spreading southeast. Potential for a period of\nthunder in the afternoon, confined to the northwest and Central\nHighlands region, as front passes over the region. Decent lapse\nrates, minimal CIN, and a couple hundred J/kg of CAPE could get\nsome cloud development and rumbles of thunder. Not anticipating\nsevere storms though.",
